==========================================================
Generic/parameters used in HDL wrapper
==========================================================


  o MG_LOGFILE_NAME. When dumping a log of the changes happening on the 
    pins of the model, MG_LOGFILE_NAME is the name of the file to write 
    this log to.  To switch this logging on, you must also set the 
    MG_MODEL_LOG environment variable to 'yes'."

  o MG_FORCED_DELAY. Obsolete as it is for built-in timing shell.

  o MG_OBJECT. This generic contains the path where the Model Manager 
    will look first to find the the shared object (.so or .sl) file for 
    the model.  By default it is the value of the DIR_<device> environment 
    variable.

  o MG_OMI_PATH. Generic only for OMI based model. This is a colon-separated 
    list of paths, which are the first set of places that the Model 
    Manager will look to find the bootstrap file for OMI dropins used in this model. 

  o MG_MSG_TIME.  When this is set to 1, the simulation time is printed with
    all messages coming from this instance of the device.  When set to -1, it
    is never printed.  When set to 0 (the default), this generic has no 
    effect (so whether or not the time is printed is determined by the dropin
    source or SWIFT/OMI runtime library, as with ModelGen);

  o MG_MSG_INSTANCE.  When this is set to 1, the path to this instance of the
    device is printed with all messages coming from this instance.  When set 
    to -1, it is never printed.  When set to 0 (the default), this generic
    has no effect (so whether or not the path is printed is determined by the
    dropin source or SWIFT/OMI runtime library, as with ModelGen);

  o MG_MSG_SEVERITY.  This can be set to "NOSEVERITY", "WARNING",
    "INFORMATIONAL", "FATAL" or "ERROR" (or any abbreviation of these), in
    which case all messages produced by this instance of the device will take
    that severity level.  If set to the empty string (or anything else), this
    generic has no effect;

  o MG_MSG_SUPPRESS.  This can be set to "NOSEVERITY", "WARNING",
    "INFORMATIONAL", "FATAL" or "ERROR" (or any abbreviation of these), in
    which case any message produced by this instance of the device which has
    a severity of less than or equal to this will be suppressed.  If set to
    the empty string (or anything else), this generic has no effect.

  o MG_MSG_V4_FORMAT.  For backward compatibility, the ModelGen message format 
    can be re-enabled by setting the new MG_MSG_V4_FORMAT generic to 1; this 
    capability may be removed in a future release;

  A few things should be noted here:

  - The above MG_MSG_* generics do not affect the messages produced by timing 
    shells.  These messages can be controlled using other generics/parameters 
    at the timing shell level;

  - Messages originating from SWIFT or OMI dropins often do not supply their
    severity information to the Model Manager.  So even though they may 
    contain words such as "WARNING" or "ERROR".  Such messages will be
    treated as "INFORMATIONAL" or "NOSEVERITY" messages as far as
    MG_MSG_SUPPRESS is concerned;

  - MG_MSG_TIME affects timestamps in message headers, not the (bogus)
    timestamps which may appear within the body of messages originating from
    SWIFT or OMI dropins.
